{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U2"],
      "pins": ["U2.1", "U2.2"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "VCC"],
      "observed": "The fixed 5 V linear regulator U2 (LM1117-5.0) has its input pin VI (pin 1) and its output pin VO (pin 2) both tied to the same +5V net (each carries a '+5V' power arrow on the drawing). The unregulated input rail (VCC, present on J3 PWR and on the pull-up/pot circuits) is not connected to the regulator input.",
      "expected": "A fixed 5 V LDO must have its input on a higher, unregulated rail (VIN/VCC) and its output on the regulated +5V net; the input and output must be separate nets so the part can drop voltage and supply the MCU. As drawn the regulator is bypassed/shorted across its own output and the +5V rail has no proper source.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U2 LM1117-5.0: pin 1 'VI' with '+5V' arrow, pin 2 'VO' with '+5V' arrow, pin 3 GND (sheet /, page 1/1, right tile)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "an ATtiny-driven fan controller (Atmel/)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The 5 V rail that powers the ATtiny (U3/U4 VCC), the potentiometer and the ISP header is not regulated. If the real supply feeding VCC is higher than 5 V, that voltage appears directly on the +5V net and can damage the MCU; if the supply is already 5 V, the regulator is a useless short and the part is mis-applied.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic shows the LM1117-5.0 regulator with its input and output both tied to the +5V net, bypassing/shorting the regulator and leaving the MCU's 5 V rail without a regulated source; this is a deviation from sound power-rail design for an ATtiny fan controller."
}

